across both segments. I should also check whether the WHIR round trees see similar improvements with the smaller query strides.\n\nLooking at the two segments at APC 300, they have different stacked widths, so segment 0 with the larger width is probably hitting that 45ms peak while segment 1 is closer to 20-30ms. If I apply the speedup, segment 0 drops to around 15ms and segment 1 to 8ms, giving me roughly 47ms in savings from Trace Commit alone, plus another 5-10ms from WHIR round trees—bringing the total improvement to about 50-60ms and reducing overall STARK time by roughly 5%.\n\nThere's another optimization I want to explore. more seriously: **can we also apply this to the `poseidon2_strided_compress_layer_kernel`?**\n\nLooking at the nsight data, I see 12.5ms total across 457 instances with an average of 27μs each. These compression layers are tiny individually, so launch overhead could account for ~2.3ms of that time. The real problem is that bottom layers process very small outputs—at APC 300, output_size is just 512, which only fills 2 blocks at 256 threads/block, leaving the GPU severely underutilized.\n\nEven a 4x speedup would only save about 8ms across all instances, which is below the noise floor. I should also check if there's relevant analysis from the failed task `2026-04-16-1300`, though I'll need to develop my own plan rather than reusing it directly. I'm leaning toward the merkle hash grid tuning approach.\n\nLet me evaluate a few other optimization candidates to be thorough: adaptive block dimensions for the poseidon2 row hashing kernel could yield 50-60ms gains with high confidence and minimal complexity; a fused multi-stage NTT kernel for the RS code matrix might save 10-20ms but requires deep CUDA kernel knowledge and significant implementation effort; and overlapping WHIR merkle tree construction with subsequent round sumcheck computation could reclaim 5-10ms, though the gains diminish for later rounds.\n\nI'm also considering profile-guided