{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013i5QYtRFY86Fp1BCSMZoi5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BMP5P1aXhHpg8FPKtN7Xh2","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-16-1600-adaptive-merkle-hash-block-size\n\nIncrease GPU SM utilization for the `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` by adaptively reducing `blockDim.x` when `query_stride` is small. At APC 300, the trace commit merkle tree hash launches only 32 blocks (25% of 128 SMs) because `query_stride=1024` with the fixed `blockDim.x=32` gives `ceil(1024/32)=32` blocks. The kernel is overwhelmingly compute-bound (53K columns × ~6625 Poseidon2 permutations per thread vs 212MB total read at 1 TB/s), so increasing block count to 128 should yield near-linear speedup on the row hashing phase — the dominant cost in Trace Commit at APC 300.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1221,"cache_read_input_tokens":121843,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1221},"output_tokens":46,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97849a12-7ea3-4743-9ec4-4f09ecc25100","uuid":"f7eaa634-952a-4f88-b814-95831ea358ff"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BMP5P1aXhHpg8FPKtN7Xh2","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97849a12-7ea3-4743-9ec4-4f09ecc25100","uuid":"54ec48bd-aedf-433f-a89b-5d84a38d5ec6","timestamp":"2026-04-16T13:51:05.911Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-16-1600-adaptive-merkle-hash-block-size\n\nIncrease GPU SM utilization for the `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` by adaptively reducing `blockDim.x` when `query_stride` is small. At APC 300, the trace commit merkle tree hash launches only 32 blocks (25% of 128 SMs) because `query_stride=1024` with the fixed `blockDim.x=32` gives `ceil(1024/32)=32` blocks. The kernel is overwhelmingly compute-bound (53K columns × ~6625 Poseidon2 permutations per thread vs 212MB total read at 1 TB/s), so increasing block count to 128 should yield near-linear speedup on the row hashing phase — the dominant cost in Trace Commit at APC 300.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-16-1430-column-batched-ntt-for-rs-code-matrix","+# 2026-04-16-1600-adaptive-merkle-hash-block-size"," ","-Improve the NTT memory access efficiency in `rs_code_matrix` by processing columns in L2-cache-sized batches instead of all columns simultaneously. The current `batch_ntt` processes all ~53K columns (at APC 300) in a single call, creating a 3.4GB working set that far exceeds the RTX 4090's 72MB L2 cache. This forces every NTT butterfly stage to read from DRAM at large strides, achieving only ~30% of peak bandwidth. By splitting the NTT into batches of ~1000 columns (~64MB per batch, fitting in L2), the second NTT stage reads data that's still L2-resident from the first stage, potentially doubling effective bandwidth. This improvement disproportionately helps APC 300 (short columns, 64KB each, excellent L2 reuse) over APC 0 (tall columns, 4MB each, less L2 benefit).","+Increase GPU SM utilization for the `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` by adaptively reducing `blockDim.x` when `query_stride` is small. At APC 300, the trace commit merkle tree hash launches only 32 blocks (25% of 128 SMs) because `query_stride=1024` with the fixed `blockDim.x=32` gives `ceil(1024/32)=32` blocks. The kernel is overwhelmingly compute-bound (53K columns × ~6625 Poseidon2 permutations per thread vs 212MB total read at 1 TB/s), so increasing block count to 128 should yield near-linear speedup on the row hashing phase — the dominant cost in Trace Commit at APC 300."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-16-1430-column-batched-ntt-for-rs-code-matrix\n\nImprove the NTT memory access efficiency in `rs_code_matrix` by processing columns in L2-cache-sized batches instead of all columns simultaneously. The current `batch_ntt` processes all ~53K columns (at APC 300) in a single call, creating a 3.4GB working set that far exceeds the RTX 4090's 72MB L2 cache. This forces every NTT butterfly stage to read from DRAM at large strides, achieving only ~30% of peak bandwidth. By splitting the NTT into batches of ~1000 columns (~64MB per batch, fitting in L2), the second NTT stage reads data that's still L2-resident from the first stage, potentially doubling effective bandwidth. This improvement disproportionately helps APC 300 (short columns, 64KB each, excellent L2 reuse) over APC 0 (tall columns, 4MB each, less L2 benefit).\n"}}
