{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J1sNLyhJZhnvdNouept5Mr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Adaptive Merkle Hash Block Size\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tImprove SM utilization for the `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` by adaptively reducing `blockDim.x` when the number of blocks would be less than 128 (the SM count on RTX 4090). This targets the dominant cost in Trace Commit at APC 300, where the kernel currently runs at 25% SM utilization.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### CUDA Kernel\n10\t\n11\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 16-79\n12\t\n13\tThe `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` does two things per block:\n14\t1. **Row hashing** (lines 39-51): Each thread (`stride_idx = blockDim.x * blockIdx.x + threadIdx.x`) iterates over all `width` columns, absorbing Fp elements into a Poseidon2 sponge with rate CELLS_RATE=8. At APC 300 with width ≈ 53K, this is ~6,625 `poseidon2_mix` calls per thread.\n15\t2. **Intra-query Merkle reduction** (lines 53-71): `log_rows_per_query` levels of tree compression in shared memory across the y-dimension (`blockDim.y = 2^log_rows_per_query = 16`).\n16\t\n17\t### Launcher\n18\t\n19\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 214-231\n20\t\n21\t```cpp\n22\textern \"C\" int _poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes(\n23\t    digest_t *out,\n24\t    const Fp *matrix,\n25\t    size_t width,\n26\t    size_t query_stride,\n27\t    size_t log_rows_per_query\n28\t) {\n29\t    auto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(query_stride, 512 >> log_rows_per_query);\n30\t    block.y = 1 << log_rows_per_query;\n31\t    size_t shared_stride = block.x * div_ceil(block.y, 2);\n32\t    size_t shmem_bytes = CELLS_OUT * shared_stride * sizeof(Fp);\n33\t    auto height = query_stride << log_rows_per_query;\n34\t\n35\t    poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel<<<grid, block, shmem_bytes>>>(\n36\t        out, matrix, width, height, query_stride, log_rows_per_query\n37\t    );\n38\t    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n39\t}\n40\t```\n41\t\n42\t`kernel_launch_params` is in `crates/cuda-common/include/launcher.cuh`, line 14:\n43\t```cpp\n44\tinline std::pair<dim3, dim3> kernel_launch_params(size_t count, size_t threads_per_block) {\n45\t    size_t block = std::min(count, threads_per_block);\n46\t    size_t grid = div_ceil(count, block);\n47\t    return std::make_pair(dim3(grid, 1, 1), dim3(block, 1, 1));\n48\t}\n49\t```\n50\t\n51\t### Current launch geometry at APC 300 (Trace Commit)\n52\t\n53\t- `log_rows_per_query` = `k_whir` = 4 (from `SystemParams::k_whir()`)\n54\t- `threads_per_block` = 512 >> 4 = 32\n55\t- `query_stride` = 1024 (codeword_height=16384 / rows_per_query=16)\n56\t- `blockDim` = (32, 16) = 512 threads/block\n57\t- `gridDim` = (ceil(1024/32), 1) = (32, 1) = **32 blocks**\n58\t- SM utilization: 32 / 128 = **25%**\n59\t- Shared memory: CELLS_OUT × (32 × 8) × sizeof(Fp) = 8 × 256 × 4 = 8,192 bytes/block\n60\t\n61\t### Current launch geometry at APC 0 (Trace Commit)\n62\t\n63\t- Same `k_whir` = 4\n64\t- `query_stride` = 65536 (codeword_height=1048576 / 16)\n65\t- `blockDim` = (32, 16) = 512 threads/block\n66\t- `gridDim` = (ceil(65536/32), 1) = (2048, 1) = **2048 blocks**\n67\t- SM utilization: 100% (2048 >> 128)\n68\t- **No change needed** — the adaptive logic skips when blocks already ≥ 128.\n69\t\n70\t### nsight kernel summary (current)\n71\t\n72\t```\n73\tposeidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel: 231ms total, 25 instances, avg 9.24ms, max 45.2ms\n74\t```\n75\t\n76\tThe 25 instances span 2 segments × (1 trace commit + N WHIR rounds) + recursion. The trace commit instances (width ≈ 53K columns, max ~45ms) dominate. WHIR round instances (width ≈ 4-16, ~2 Poseidon2 mixes per thread) are lightweight and won't benefit significantly.\n77\t\n78\t### Callers\n79\t\n80\t1. `stacked_commit()` (`stacked_pcs.rs:48-84`) → `MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash()` → `new_generic_with_hash()` (`merkle_tree.rs:113-172`) → `MH::compress_rows()` → `_poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes`\n81\t   - Called once per segment during Trace Commit. This is the high-impact path.\n82\t\n83\t2. `prove_whir_opening_gpu()` (`whir.rs:318-326`) → `MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash()` → same path\n84\t   - Called per WHIR round. Width is 4 (extension field elements = 16 Fp elements). Marginal benefit.\n85\t\n86\t3. Extension variant `_poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_ext` (`merkle_tree.cu:233-250`) — same launcher pattern, same fix needed.\n87\t\n88\t### Compute vs memory analysis\n89\t\n90\tAt APC 300 Trace Commit:\n91\t- Each thread reads `width` × 4 bytes = 53K × 4 = 212 KB from column-major matrix\n92\t- Total memory read: 1024 threads × 212 KB = 212 MB → 0.2 ms at 1 TB/s\n93\t- Each thread does ~6,625 Poseidon2 mixes → ~9.3 ms at 100% utilization\n94\t- Kernel takes ~40 ms at 25% utilization\n95\t- **Compute:memory ratio > 200:1** — purely compute-bound\n96\t\n97\t## Changes\n98\t\n99\t### Change 1: Add adaptive blockDim.x in the Fp hash launcher\n100\t\n101\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 214-231\n102\t\n103\tReplace the fixed `kernel_launch_params(query_stride, 512 >> log_rows_per_query)` with adaptive logic that reduces `blockDim.x` until the grid has at least `MIN_BLOCKS` blocks:\n104\t\n105\t```cpp\n106\textern \"C\" int _poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes(\n107\t    digest_t *out,\n108\t    const Fp *matrix,\n109\t    size_t width,\n110\t    size_t query_stride,\n111\t    size_t log_rows_per_query\n112\t) {\n113\t    // Adaptively reduce blockDim.x to achieve >= MIN_BLOCKS blocks,\n114\t    // ensuring full SM utilization on GPUs with 128 SMs.\n115\t    constexpr size_t MIN_BLOCKS = 128;\n116\t    size_t default_bx = 512 >> log_rows_per_query;\n117\t    size_t bx = default_bx;\n118\t    while (bx > 1 && div_ceil(query_stride, bx) < MIN_BLOCKS) {\n119\t        bx >>= 1;\n120\t    }\n121\t    auto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(query_stride, bx);\n122\t    block.y = 1 << log_rows_per_query;\n123\t    size_t shared_stride = block.x * div_ceil(block.y, 2);\n124\t    size_t shmem_bytes = CELLS_OUT * shared_stride * sizeof(Fp);\n125\t    auto height = query_stride << log_rows_per_query;\n126\t\n127\t    poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel<<<grid, block, shmem_bytes>>>(\n128\t        out, matrix, width, height, query_stride, log_rows_per_query\n129\t    );\n130\t    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n131\t}\n132\t```\n133\t\n134\t**New geometry at APC 300**: bx halves from 32→16→8 (stopping when ceil(1024/8)=128 ≥ 128). `blockDim=(8,16)=128 threads/block`, `gridDim=(128,1)`. All 128 SMs utilized.\n135\t\n136\t**Shared memory at new geometry**: CELLS_OUT × (8 × 8) × sizeof(Fp) = 8 × 64 × 4 = 2,048 bytes/block. Well within 48 KB limit.\n137\t\n138\t**APC 0 is untouched**: default_bx=32, div_ceil(65536,32)=2048 ≥ 128, loop doesn't fire.\n139\t\n140\t### Change 2: Apply the same adaptive logic to the ext variant\n141\t\n142\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 233-250\n143\t\n144\tApply the identical `MIN_BLOCKS` adaptive loop to `_poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_ext`. The ext variant is used for extension-field merkle trees. Same while loop, same MIN_BLOCKS constant.\n145\t\n146\t### No kernel changes required\n147\t\n148\tThe kernel itself (`poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel`) is unmodified. The per-thread computation is identical regardless of `blockDim.x`. The shared memory Merkle reduction (lines 53-71) uses `shared_offset = ((leaf_idx >> (layer+1)) << layer) * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x` which stays within bounds at any blockDim.x ≥ 1.\n149\t\n150\tThe `__syncthreads()` barriers (lines 61, 71) remain correct because they synchronize all threads within a block, and the shared memory accesses are bounded by `shared_stride = blockDim.x * (blockDim.y >> 1)`.\n151\t\n152\t## Invariants\n153\t\n154\t1. **Correctness**: Each thread processes exactly one stride position (`stride_idx = blockDim.x * blockIdx.x + threadIdx.x`). Total thread count = grid.x × block.x × block.y = query_stride × rows_per_query = height. Changing blockDim.x only redistributes stride positions across blocks; per-thread computation is unchanged. The output is bit-identical.\n155\t\n156\t2. **APC 0 no-regression**: When query_stride is large (≥ default_bx × MIN_BLOCKS), the while loop exits immediately and the launch geometry is unchanged from today.\n157\t\n158\t3. **Shared memory bounds**: `shared_stride = block.x × div_ceil(block.y, 2)`. The reduction tree accesses at most `CELLS_OUT × shared_stride` Fp elements. With smaller block.x, shared_stride shrinks, but all indices (`shared_offset < shared_stride`) remain within bounds.\n159\t\n160\t4. **Thread count**: grid.x × block.x × block.y = query_stride × (1 << log_rows_per_query) = height. This is preserved regardless of block.x choice.\n161\t\n162\t5. **No VPMM pool state impact**: This change does not alter any GPU memory allocation patterns. It only changes kernel launch geometry. The VPMM pool state sensitivity (observed in multiple previous tasks) is not a concern.\n163\t\n164\t## Measurement Plan\n165\t\n166\t1. Run `PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics metrics.json --recursion` for APC 0, 100, 300 before and after.\n167\t2. Compare `STARK (excl. trace)` and `Trace Commit` via `spec.py`.\n168\t3. Run nsight profile on APC 300 after the change to verify:\n169\t   - `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` instance count is unchanged (25)\n170\t   - Grid dimensions increased from 32 to 128 for trace commit instances\n171\t   - Total kernel GPU time decreased (231ms → ~120-150ms expected)\n172\t4. APC 0 must show no regression (within ±20ms noise on STARK excl trace).\n173\t\n174\t### Expected results\n175\t\n176\t- Trace Commit APC 300: 197ms → ~145-160ms (hash portion ~40ms/seg → ~13ms/seg)\n177\t- STARK excl trace APC 300: 1101ms → ~1045-1065ms (~3-5% improvement, ~2.30x vs baseline)\n178\t- APC 0: no change (2048 blocks already saturate 128 SMs)\n179\t\n180\t## Rollback Criteria\n181\t\n182\t- Less than 20ms improvement on STARK excl trace at APC 300.\n183\t- Any regression > 20ms on STARK excl trace at APC 0.\n184\t- Any correctness failure (proof verification fails at any APC configuration).\n185\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FzJCGfuL1RSheRVC5YTeLs","session_id":"97849a12-7ea3-4743-9ec4-4f09ecc25100","uuid":"3691c29e-9f42-42f8-8db5-e3873045d959","timestamp":"2026-04-16T13:52:48.803Z"}
