HIR round 0 NTT is the biggest bottleneck at around 54ms total, though it's bandwidth-limited. The trace commit bit reversal could save 10-12ms if I integrate it into the NTT kernel itself. There's also a 30ms gap between GPU execution time and wall clock time in the GKR fractional sumcheck that comes from data transfers and CPU overhead—if I could keep the challenges on GPU instead of moving them back and forth, that could be significant.\n\nLet me focus on finding CPU-side overhead that's either redundant or could be overlapped with GPU work. Looking at the constraint phase breakdown, the 367ms for rap_constraints in segment 0 accounts for round 0 (90ms), logup_gkr (193ms), and mle_rounds (84ms), which adds up cleanly with no hidden gaps.\n\nFor the openings phase, stacked_reduction takes 37ms and whir takes 65ms, totaling 102ms against the reported 103ms—just a 1ms variance. The trace commit itself is 106ms with merkle_tree at 3ms, leaving about 103ms for the rs_code_matrix computation.\n\nI'm settling on multi-streaming the Stacked Reduction Round 0 block sum computations across 2-4 threads with separate CUDA streams. At APC 300, there are many traces per segment, and the per-trace block sum computations in Round 0 (14ms for seg0, 12ms for seg1) could benefit from parallelization across threads rather than sequential processing.\n\nHowever, I'm running into a problem—a previous attempt at multi-streaming stacked reduction failed because buffer allocations and frees through the CUDA memory pool corrupted the pool state. The difference here is that I'd target only Round 0 with pre-allocated buffers instead of the entire stacked reduction pipeline. But even then, Round 0 is just 14ms per segment, and multi-streaming 797 tiny kernels (~28μs each) across 8 threads might only save 10-12ms while the threading overhead could eat into those gains.\n\nLet me do the math more carefully: 797 kernels at ~28μs each is 22ms total, or ~11ms per segment. Multi-streaming across 8