n};\n\n// Batched kernel: processes all traces of the same height in one launch.\n// Each block processes one trace in the descriptor array.\n// Uses atomicAdd to accumulate into shared G output buffer.\n__global__ void batched_stacked_reduction_round0_kernel(\n    const FpExt *eq_r_ns,\n    const StackedR0Desc *descs,\n    const uint32_t *block_starts,  // prefix sum: which desc this block belongs to\n    uint32_t num_descs,\n    FpExt *block_sums,    // [total_blocks * NUM_G * skip_domain]\n    uint32_t trace_height, // same for all in this batch\n    uint32_t l_skip\n);\n```\n\nThe existing per-trace kernel body can be reused: each block computes partial G sums for its assigned trace, writing to its segment of block_sums. A final reduction kernel sums all block_sums into the G output.\n\n**Key design**: Use the `BlockCtx` binary-search pattern from the batch MLE kernel (`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`) to map blocks to descriptors.\n\n### Change 2: Add batched PLE fold CUDA kernel\n\nSame approach: descriptor array with per-trace src/dst pointers.\n\n```c\nstruct FoldPleDesc {\n    const Fp *src;        // trace buffer\n    FpExt *dst;           // output buffer (already offset)\n    uint32_t trace_width;\n};\n\n__global__ void batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel(\n    const FoldPleDesc *descs,\n    const uint32_t *block_starts,\n    uint32_t num_descs,\n    const Fp *omega_skip_pows,\n    const FpExt *inv_lagrange_denoms,\n    uint32_t trace_height,\n    uint32_t l_skip\n);\n```\n\n### Change 3: Add FFI bindings\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nAdd `repr(C)` structs for `StackedR0Desc` and `FoldPleDesc`, plus extern \"C\" declarations and safe wrapper functions.\n\n### Change 4: Orchestration in Rust\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nIn `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`:\n1. Group `trace_ptrs` by (trace_height, sign_of_n) — traces that would accumulate into the same G buffer with the same kernel configuration.\n2. For each group: build descriptor array on host, upload to device, launch batched kernel.\n3. Use existing final_reduce pattern to accumulate block sums into G buffers.\n\nIn `fold_ple_evals`:\n1. Group traces by trace_height.\n2. For each group: build descriptor array, upload, launch batched kernel.\n\n**Threshold**: Only use batched path when group has ≥ 10 traces. Below that, the descriptor overhead exceeds the launch savings.\n\n### Change 5: Pre-allocate block_sums buffer\n\nCurrently, `self.d_block_sums` is resized per trace (line 529-531). With batched launches, pre-allocate a block_sums buffer large enough for the largest batch at the start of round 0.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Each trace in the batch produces the same G0/G1/G2 partial sums as the per-trace path. The final reduction accumulates them identically. The bitwise output is identical to the sequential path (modulo floating point associativity, which doesn't apply to finite field arithmetic).\n\n2. **APC 0 no-regression**: At APC 0, there are ~20 traces per segment. Most groups will have < 10 traces, falling below the batching threshold. The sequential path runs unchanged.\n\n3. **Output buffer safety**: The round 0 batched kernel writes to block_sums (not directly to G output). The final reduce kernel adds block_sums into G output. This matches the current per-trace pattern where block_sums is a temporary buffer.\n\n4. **No VPMM pool impact**: The descriptor arrays are small (400 descriptors × 24 bytes = ~10 KB). The block_sums pre-allocation replaces per-trace resizing with a single upfront allocation. Net memory impact is negligible.\n\n5. **fold_ple output correctness**: Each trace's folded output is written to a non-overlapping region of the q_evals buffer (indexed by dst_offset). The batched kernel uses per-descriptor dst pointers that match the sequential per-trace offsets exactly.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Run benchmark for APC 0, 100, 300 before and after the change.\n2. Compare `prover.openings.stacked_reduction.round0_time_ms` per segment.\n3. Compare `STARK (excl. trace)` overall.\n4. Run nsight profile on APC 300 to verify:\n   - `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel` instance count decreased (from ~797 to ~80-100)\n   - Total kernel GPU time unchanged or slightly improved\n   - No new kernel time regression in other phases\n5. APC 0 must show no regression (within ±10ms noise).\n\n### Expected results\n\n- Stacked Reduction Round 0 per segment: 14ms → 10-12ms (seg0), 12ms → 9-11ms (seg1)\n- STARK excl trace APC 300: 1101ms → ~1087-1093ms (8-14ms improvement)\n- APC 0: no change (below batching threshold)\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- Less than 5ms improvement on stacked reduction round 0 total at APC 300.\n- Any regression > 10ms on STARK excl trace at APC 0.\n- Any correctness failure (proof verification fails at any APC configuration).\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":184,"newStart":1,"newLines":190,"lines":["-# Plan: Adaptive Merkle Hash Block Size","+# Plan: Batch Stacked Reduction Round 0 Descriptor Arrays"," "," ## Goal"," ","-Improve SM utilization for the `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` by adaptively reducing `blockDim.x` when the number of blocks would be less than 128 (the SM count on RTX 4090). This targets the dominant cost in Trace Commit at APC 300, where the kernel currently runs at 25% SM utilization.","+Reduce kernel launch overhead in the stacked reduction Round 0 by batching per-trace kernel launches into per-height-group batched launches using descriptor arrays. At APC 300, the stacked reduction Round 0 calls `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` and `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` once per backing trace matrix (~400 per segment across all commits), with many being tiny (1-2 columns, height 2^5-2^8). By grouping same-height traces into single batched kernel launches, we reduce ~800 kernel launches per segment to ~80, saving ~720 launches × ~5µs = ~3.6ms per segment."," "," ## Current Code Path"," ","-### CUDA Kernel","+### Round 0 sumcheck loop"," ","-**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 16-79","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, lines 504-549"," ","-The `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` does two things per block:","-1. **Row hashing** (lines 39-51): Each thread (`stride_idx = blockDim.x * blockIdx.x + threadIdx.x`) iterates over all `width` columns, absorbing Fp elements into a Poseidon2 sponge with rate CELLS_RATE=8. At APC 300 with width ≈ 53K, this is ~6,625 `poseidon2_mix` calls per thread.","-2. **Intra-query Merkle reduction** (lines 53-71): `log_rows_per_query` levels of tree compression in shared memory across the y-dimension (`blockDim.y = 2^log_rows_per_query = 16`).","-","-### Launcher","-","-**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 214-231","-","-```cpp","-extern \"C\" int _poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes(","-    digest_t *out,","-    const Fp *matrix,","-    size_t width,","-    size_t query_stride,","-    size_t log_rows_per_query","+```rust","+for ((trace_ptr, trace_height, trace_width), window) in zip(","+    mem::take(&mut self.trace_ptrs),","+    self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2),"," ) {","-    auto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(query_stride, 512 >> log_rows_per_query);","-    block.y = 1 << log_rows_per_query;","-    size_t shared_stride = block.x * div_ceil(block.y, 2);","-    size_t shmem_bytes = CELLS_OUT * shared_stride * sizeof(Fp);","-    auto height = query_stride << log_rows_per_query;","+    let n = log_height as isize - l_skip as isize;","+    let d_g_output = if n >= 0 { &mut d_g_pos } else { &mut d_g_neg[(-n-1) as usize] };"," ","-    poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel<<<grid, block, shmem_bytes>>>(","-        out, matrix, width, height, query_stride, log_rows_per_query","+    // Per-trace temp buffer","+    if block_sums_len > self.d_block_sums.len() {","+        self.d_block_sums = DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(block_sums_len);","+    }","+","+    stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0(","+        &self.eq_r_ns, trace_ptr, lambda_pows_ptr,","+        &mut self.d_block_sums, d_g_output,","+        trace_height, trace_width, l_skip,","     );","-    return CHECK_KERNEL();"," }"," ```"," ","-`kernel_launch_params` is in `crates/cuda-common/include/launcher.cuh`, line 14:","-```cpp","-inline std::pair<dim3, dim3> kernel_launch_params(size_t count, size_t threads_per_block) {","-    size_t block = std::min(count, threads_per_block);","-    size_t grid = div_ceil(count, block);","-    return std::make_pair(dim3(grid, 1, 1), dim3(block, 1, 1));","+Each call launches a CUDA kernel that computes G0, G1, G2 partial sums for one trace matrix, then a final reduction kernel that ADDs block sums into the output bucket.","+","+### PLE fold loop","+","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, lines 714-743","+","+```rust","+for trace in &stacked.traces {","+    stacked_reduction_fold_ple(","+        trace.buffer().as_ptr(), dst,","+        &self.d_omega_skip_pows, &d_inv_lagrange_denoms,","+        trace.height(), trace.width(), l_skip,","+    );","+    dst_offset += new_height * trace.width();"," }"," ```"," ","-### Current launch geometry at APC 300 (Trace Commit)","+Each call launches one CUDA kernel that folds a single trace's PLE evaluations."," ","-- `log_rows_per_query` = `k_whir` = 4 (from `SystemParams::k_whir()`)","-- `threads_per_block` = 512 >> 4 = 32","-- `query_stride` = 1024 (codeword_height=16384 / rows_per_query=16)","-- `blockDim` = (32, 16) = 512 threads/block","-- `gridDim` = (ceil(1024/32), 1) = (32, 1) = **32 blocks**","-- SM utilization: 32 / 128 = **25%**","-- Shared memory: CELLS_OUT × (32 × 8) × sizeof(Fp) = 8 × 256 × 4 = 8,192 bytes/block","+### CUDA kernel signatures"," ","-### Current launch geometry at APC 0 (Trace Commit)","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`, lines 44-63"," ","-- Same `k_whir` = 4","-- `query_stride` = 65536 (codeword_height=1048576 / 16)","-- `blockDim` = (32, 16) = 512 threads/block","-- `gridDim` = (ceil(65536/32), 1) = (2048, 1) = **2048 blocks**","-- SM utilization: 100% (2048 >> 128)","-- **No change needed** — the adaptive logic skips when blocks already ≥ 128.","+```c","+// Round 0: computes G0, G1, G2 block sums + final reduction","+_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0(","+    eq_r_ns, trace_ptr, lambda_pows, block_sums, output,","+    trace_height, trace_width, l_skip, num_x);"," ","-### nsight kernel summary (current)","-","+// PLE fold: folds trace evals by Lagrange interpolation at u_0","+_stacked_reduction_fold_ple(","+    src, dst, omega_skip_pows, inv_lagrange_denoms,","+    trace_height, trace_width, l_skip);"," ```","-poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel: 231ms total, 25 instances, avg 9.24ms, max 45.2ms","-```"," ","-The 25 instances span 2 segments × (1 trace commit + N WHIR rounds) + recursion. The trace commit instances (width ≈ 53K columns, max ~45ms) dominate. WHIR round instances (width ≈ 4-16, ~2 Poseidon2 mixes per thread) are lightweight and won't benefit significantly.","+### nsight kernel data (APC 300)"," ","-### Callers","+- `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel`: 22ms total, 797 instances, avg 28µs","+- `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel`: 9ms total, 797 instances, avg 11µs","+- Total: 31ms GPU, ~26ms wall clock (37ms + 36ms for 2 segments minus MLE portion)","+- Per-segment round 0 wall clock: 14ms (seg0), 12ms (seg1) = 26ms total","+- The ~400 launches per segment include traces from common_main + preprocessed + cached_main commits"," ","-1. `stacked_commit()` (`stacked_pcs.rs:48-84`) → `MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash()` → `new_generic_with_hash()` (`merkle_tree.rs:113-172`) → `MH::compress_rows()` → `_poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes`","-   - Called once per segment during Trace Commit. This is the high-impact path.","+## Changes"," ","-2. `prove_whir_opening_gpu()` (`whir.rs:318-326`) → `MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash()` → same path","-   - Called per WHIR round. Width is 4 (extension field elements = 16 Fp elements). Marginal benefit.","+### Change 1: Add batched Round 0 CUDA kernel"," ","-3. Extension variant `_poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_ext` (`merkle_tree.cu:233-250`) — same launcher pattern, same fix needed.","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu` (or a new file `stacked_reduction_batched.cu`)"," ","-### Compute vs memory analysis","+Add a descriptor struct and batched kernel:"," ","-At APC 300 Trace Commit:","-- Each thread reads `width` × 4 bytes = 53K × 4 = 212 KB from column-major matrix","-- Total memory read: 1024 threads × 212 KB = 212 MB → 0.2 ms at 1 TB/s","-- Each thread does ~6,625 Poseidon2 mixes → ~9.3 ms at 100% utilization","-- Kernel takes ~40 ms at 25% utilization","-- **Compute:memory ratio > 200:1** — purely compute-bound","+```c","+struct StackedR0Desc {","+    const Fp *trace_ptr;","+    const FpExt *lambda_pows;  // already offset to this trace's position","+    uint32_t trace_width;","+};"," ","-## Changes","+// Batched kernel: processes all traces of the same height in one launch.","+// Each block processes one trace in the descriptor array.","+// Uses atomicAdd to accumulate into shared G output buffer.","+__global__ void batched_stacked_reduction_round0_kernel(","+    const FpExt *eq_r_ns,","+    const StackedR0Desc *descs,","+    const uint32_t *block_starts,  // prefix sum: which desc this block belongs to","+    uint32_t num_descs,","+    FpExt *block_sums,    // [total_blocks * NUM_G * skip_domain]","+    uint32_t trace_height, // same for all in this batch","+    uint32_t l_skip","+);","+```"," ","-### Change 1: Add adaptive blockDim.x in the Fp hash launcher","+The existing per-trace kernel body can be reused: each block computes partial G sums for its assigned trace, writing to its segment of block_sums. A final reduction kernel sums all block_sums into the G output."," ","-**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 214-231","+**Key design**: Use the `BlockCtx` binary-search pattern from the batch MLE kernel (`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`) to map blocks to descriptors."," ","-Replace the fixed `kernel_launch_params(query_stride, 512 >> log_rows_per_query)` with adaptive logic that reduces `blockDim.x` until the grid has at least `MIN_BLOCKS` blocks:","+### Change 2: Add batched PLE fold CUDA kernel"," ","-```cpp","-extern \"C\" int _poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes(","-    digest_t *out,","-    const Fp *matrix,","-    size_t width,","-    size_t query_stride,","-    size_t log_rows_per_query","-) {","-    // Adaptively reduce blockDim.x to achieve >= MIN_BLOCKS blocks,","-    // ensuring full SM utilization on GPUs with 128 SMs.","-    constexpr size_t MIN_BLOCKS = 128;","-    size_t default_bx = 512 >> log_rows_per_query;","-    size_t bx = default_bx;","-    while (bx > 1 && div_ceil(query_stride, bx) < MIN_BLOCKS) {","-        bx >>= 1;","-    }","-    auto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(query_stride, bx);","-    block.y = 1 << log_rows_per_query;","-    size_t shared_stride = block.x * div_ceil(block.y, 2);","-    size_t shmem_bytes = CELLS_OUT * shared_stride * sizeof(Fp);","-    auto height = query_stride << log_rows_per_query;","+Same approach: descriptor array with per-trace src/dst pointers."," ","-    poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel<<<grid, block, shmem_bytes>>>(","-        out, matrix, width, height, query_stride, log_rows_per_query","-    );","-    return CHECK_KERNEL();","-}","+```c","+struct FoldPleDesc {","+    const Fp *src;        // trace buffer","+    FpExt *dst;           // output buffer (already offset)","+    uint32_t trace_width;","+};","+","+__global__ void batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel(","+    const FoldPleDesc *descs,","+    const uint32_t *block_starts,","+    uint32_t num_descs,","+    const Fp *omega_skip_pows,","+    const FpExt *inv_lagrange_denoms,","+    uint32_t trace_height,","+    uint32_t l_skip","+);"," ```"," ","-**New geometry at APC 300**: bx halves from 32→16→8 (stopping when ceil(1024/8)=128 ≥ 128). `blockDim=(8,16)=128 threads/block`, `gridDim=(128,1)`. All 128 SMs utilized.","+### Change 3: Add FFI bindings"," ","-**Shared memory at new geometry**: CELLS_OUT × (8 × 8) × sizeof(Fp) = 8 × 64 × 4 = 2,048 bytes/block. Well within 48 KB limit.","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`"," ","-**APC 0 is untouched**: default_bx=32, div_ceil(65536,32)=2048 ≥ 128, loop doesn't fire.","+Add `repr(C)` structs for `StackedR0Desc` and `FoldPleDesc`, plus extern \"C\" declarations and safe wrapper functions."," ","-### Change 2: Apply the same adaptive logic to the ext variant","+### Change 4: Orchestration in Rust"," ","-**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 233-250","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`"," ","-Apply the identical `MIN_BLOCKS` adaptive loop to `_poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_ext`. The ext variant is used for extension-field merkle trees. Same while loop, same MIN_BLOCKS constant.","+In `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`:","+1. Group `trace_ptrs` by (trace_height, sign_of_n) — traces that would accumulate into the same G buffer with the same kernel configuration.","+2. For each group: build descriptor array on host, upload to device, launch batched kernel.","+3. Use existing final_reduce pattern to accumulate block sums into G buffers."," ","-### No kernel changes required","+In `fold_ple_evals`:","+1. Group traces by trace_height.","+2. For each group: build descriptor array, upload, launch batched kernel."," ","-The kernel itself (`poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel`) is unmodified. The per-thread computation is identical regardless of `blockDim.x`. The shared memory Merkle reduction (lines 53-71) uses `shared_offset = ((leaf_idx >> (layer+1)) << layer) * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x` which stays within bounds at any blockDim.x ≥ 1.","+**Threshold**: Only use batched path when group has ≥ 10 traces. Below that, the descriptor overhead exceeds the launch savings."," ","-The `__syncthreads()` barriers (lines 61, 71) remain correct because they synchronize all threads within a block, and the shared memory accesses are bounded by `shared_stride = blockDim.x * (blockDim.y >> 1)`.","+### Change 5: Pre-allocate block_sums buffer"," ","+Currently, `self.d_block_sums` is resized per trace (line 529-531). With batched launches, pre-allocate a block_sums buffer large enough for the largest batch at the start of round 0.","+"," ## Invariants"," ","-1. **Correctness**: Each thread processes exactly one stride position (`stride_idx = blockDim.x * blockIdx.x + threadIdx.x`). Total thread count = grid.x × block.x × block.y = query_stride × rows_per_query = height. Changing blockDim.x only redistributes stride positions across blocks; per-thread computation is unchanged. The output is bit-identical.","+1. **Correctness**: Each trace in the batch produces the same G0/G1/G2 partial sums as the per-trace path. The final reduction accumulates them identically. The bitwise output is identical to the sequential path (modulo floating point associativity, which doesn't apply to finite field arithmetic)."," ","-2. **APC 0 no-regression**: When query_stride is large (≥ default_bx × MIN_BLOCKS), the while loop exits immediately and the launch geometry is unchanged from today.","+2. **APC 0 no-regression**: At APC 0, there are ~20 traces per segment. Most groups will have < 10 traces, falling below the batching threshold. The sequential path runs unchanged."," ","-3. **Shared memory bounds**: `shared_stride = block.x × div_ceil(block.y, 2)`. The reduction tree accesses at most `CELLS_OUT × shared_stride` Fp elements. With smaller block.x, shared_stride shrinks, but all indices (`shared_offset < shared_stride`) remain within bounds.","+3. **Output buffer safety**: The round 0 batched kernel writes to block_sums (not directly to G output). The final reduce kernel adds block_sums into G output. This matches the current per-trace pattern where block_sums is a temporary buffer."," ","-4. **Thread count**: grid.x × block.x × block.y = query_stride × (1 << log_rows_per_query) = height. This is preserved regardless of block.x choice.","+4. **No VPMM pool impact**: The descriptor arrays are small (400 descriptors × 24 bytes = ~10 KB). The block_sums pre-allocation replaces per-trace resizing with a single upfront allocation. Net memory impact is negligible."," ","-5. **No VPMM pool state impact**: This change does not alter any GPU memory allocation patterns. It only changes kernel launch geometry. The VPMM pool state sensitivity (observed in multiple previous tasks) is not a concern.","+5. **fold_ple output correctness**: Each trace's folded output is written to a non-overlapping region of the q_evals buffer (indexed by dst_offset). The batched kernel uses per-descriptor dst pointers that match the sequential per-trace offsets exactly."," "," ## Measurement Plan"," ","-1. Run `PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics metrics.json --recursion` for APC 0, 100, 300 before and after.","-2. Compare `STARK (excl. trace)` and `Trace Commit` via `spec.py`.","-3. Run nsight profile on APC 300 after the change to verify:","-   - `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` instance count is unchanged (25)","-   - Grid dimensions increased from 32 to 128 for trace commit instances","-   - Total kernel GPU time decreased (231ms → ~120-150ms expected)","-4. APC 0 must show no regression (within ±20ms noise on STARK excl trace).","+1. Run benchmark for APC 0, 100, 300 before and after the change.","+2. Compare `prover.openings.stacked_reduction.round0_time_ms` per segment.","+3. Compare `STARK (excl. trace)` overall.","+4. Run nsight profile on APC 300 to verify:","+   - `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel` instance count decreased (from ~797 to ~80-100)","+   - Total kernel GPU time unchanged or slightly improved","+   - No new kernel time regression in other phases","+5. APC 0 must show no regression (within ±10ms noise)."," "," ### Expected results"," ","-- Trace Commit APC 300: 197ms → ~145-160ms (hash portion ~40ms/seg → ~13ms/seg)","-- STARK excl trace APC 300: 1101ms → ~1045-1065ms (~3-5% improvement, ~2.30x vs baseline)","-- APC 0: no change (2048 blocks already saturate 128 SMs)","+- Stacked Reduction Round 0 per segment: 14ms → 10-12ms (seg0), 12ms → 9-11ms (seg1)","+- STARK excl trace APC 300: 1101ms → ~1087-1093ms (8-14ms improvement)","+- APC 0: no change (below batching threshold)"," "," ## Rollback Criteria"," ","-- Less than 20ms improvement on STARK excl trace at APC 300.","-- Any regression > 20ms on STARK excl trace at APC 0.","+- Less than 5ms improvement on stacked reduction round 0 total at APC 300.","+- Any regression > 10ms on STARK excl trace at APC 0."," - Any correctness failure (proof verification fails at any APC configuration)."]}],"originalFile":"# Plan: Adaptive Merkle Hash Block Size\n\n## Goal\n\nImprove SM utilization for the `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` by adaptively reducing `blockDim.x` when the number of blocks would be less than 128 (the SM count on RTX 4090). This targets the dominant cost in Trace Commit at APC 300, where the kernel currently runs at 25% SM utilization.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### CUDA Kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 16-79\n\nThe `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` does two things per block:\n1. **Row hashing** (lines 39-51): Each thread (`stride_idx = blockDim.x * blockIdx.x + threadIdx.x`) iterates over all `width` columns, absorbing Fp elements into a Poseidon2 sponge with rate CELLS_RATE=8. At APC 300 with width ≈ 53K, this is ~6,625 `poseidon2_mix` calls per thread.\n2. **Intra-query Merkle reduction** (lines 53-71): `log_rows_per_query` levels of tree compression in shared memory across the y-dimension (`blockDim.y = 2^log_rows_per_query = 16`).\n\n### Launcher\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 214-231\n\n```cpp\nextern \"C\" int _poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes(\n    digest_t *out,\n    const Fp *matrix,\n    size_t width,\n    size_t query_stride,\n    size_t log_rows_per_query\n) {\n    auto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(query_stride, 512 >> log_rows_per_query);\n    block.y = 1 << log_rows_per_query;\n    size_t shared_stride = block.x * div_ceil(block.y, 2);\n    size_t shmem_bytes = CELLS_OUT * shared_stride * sizeof(Fp);\n    auto height = query_stride << log_rows_per_query;\n\n    poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel<<<grid, block, shmem_bytes>>>(\n        out, matrix, width, height, query_stride, log_rows_per_query\n    );\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n`kernel_launch_params` is in `crates/cuda-common/include/launcher.cuh`, line 14:\n```cpp\ninline std::pair<dim3, dim3> kernel_launch_params(size_t count, size_t threads_per_block) {\n    size_t block = std::min(count, threads_per_block);\n    size_t grid = div_ceil(count, block);\n    return std::make_pair(dim3(grid, 1, 1), dim3(block, 1, 1));\n}\n```\n\n### Current launch geometry at APC 300 (Trace Commit)\n\n- `log_rows_per_query` = `k_whir` = 4 (from `SystemParams::k_whir()`)\n- `threads_per_block` = 512 >> 4 = 32\n- `query_stride` = 1024 (codeword_height=16384 / rows_per_query=16)\n- `blockDim` = (32, 16) = 512 threads/block\n- `gridDim` = (ceil(1024/32), 1) = (32, 1) = **32 blocks**\n- SM utilization: 32 / 128 = **25%**\n- Shared memory: CELLS_OUT × (32 × 8) × sizeof(Fp) = 8 × 256 × 4 = 8,192 bytes/block\n\n### Current launch geometry at APC 0 (Trace Commit)\n\n- Same `k_whir` = 4\n- `query_stride` = 65536 (codeword_height=1048576 / 16)\n- `blockDim` = (32, 16) = 512 threads/block\n- `gridDim` = (ceil(65536/32), 1) = (2048, 1) = **2048 blocks**\n- SM utilization: 100% (2048 >> 128)\n- **No change needed** — the adaptive logic skips when blocks already ≥ 128.\n\n### nsight kernel summary (current)\n\n```\nposeidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel: 231ms total, 25 instances, avg 9.24ms, max 45.2ms\n```\n\nThe 25 instances span 2 segments × (1 trace commit + N WHIR rounds) + recursion. The trace commit instances (width ≈ 53K columns, max ~45ms) dominate. WHIR round instances (width ≈ 4-16, ~2 Poseidon2 mixes per thread) are lightweight and won't benefit significantly.\n\n### Callers\n\n1. `stacked_commit()` (`stacked_pcs.rs:48-84`) → `MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash()` → `new_generic_with_hash()` (`merkle_tree.rs:113-172`) → `MH::compress_rows()` → `_poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes`\n   - Called once per segment during Trace Commit. This is the high-impact path.\n\n2. `prove_whir_opening_gpu()` (`whir.rs:318-326`) → `MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash()` → same path\n   - Called per WHIR round. Width is 4 (extension field elements = 16 Fp elements). Marginal benefit.\n\n3. Extension variant `_poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_ext` (`merkle_tree.cu:233-250`) — same launcher pattern, same fix needed.\n\n### Compute vs memory analysis\n\nAt APC 300 Trace Commit:\n- Each thread reads `width` × 4 bytes = 53K × 4 = 212 KB from column-major matrix\n- Total memory read: 1024 threads × 212 KB = 212 MB → 0.2 ms at 1 TB/s\n- Each thread does ~6,625 Poseidon2 mixes → ~9.3 ms at 100% utilization\n- Kernel takes ~40 ms at 25% utilization\n- **Compute:memory ratio > 200:1** — purely compute-bound\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Add adaptive blockDim.x in the Fp hash launcher\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 214-231\n\nReplace the fixed `kernel_launch_params(query_stride, 512 >> log_rows_per_query)` with adaptive logic that reduces `blockDim.x` until the grid has at least `MIN_BLOCKS` blocks:\n\n```cpp\nextern \"C\" int _poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes(\n    digest_t *out,\n    const Fp *matrix,\n    size_t width,\n    size_t query_stride,\n    size_t log_rows_per_query\n) {\n    // Adaptively reduce blockDim.x to achieve >= MIN_BLOCKS blocks,\n    // ensuring full SM utilization on GPUs with 128 SMs.\n    constexpr size_t MIN_BLOCKS = 128;\n    size_t default_bx = 512 >> log_rows_per_query;\n    size_t bx = default_bx;\n    while (bx > 1 && div_ceil(query_stride, bx) < MIN_BLOCKS) {\n        bx >>= 1;\n    }\n    auto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(query_stride, bx);\n    block.y = 1 << log_rows_per_query;\n    size_t shared_stride = block.x * div_ceil(block.y, 2);\n    size_t shmem_bytes = CELLS_OUT * shared_stride * sizeof(Fp);\n    auto height = query_stride << log_rows_per_query;\n\n    poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel<<<grid, block, shmem_bytes>>>(\n        out, matrix, width, height, query_stride, log_rows_per_query\n    );\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n**New geometry at APC 300**: bx halves from 32→16→8 (stopping when ceil(1024/8)=128 ≥ 128). `blockDim=(8,16)=128 threads/block`, `gridDim=(128,1)`. All 128 SMs utilized.\n\n**Shared memory at new geometry**: CELLS_OUT × (8 × 8) × sizeof(Fp) = 8 × 64 × 4 = 2,048 bytes/block. Well within 48 KB limit.\n\n**APC 0 is untouched**: default_bx=32, div_ceil(65536,32)=2048 ≥ 128, loop doesn't fire.\n\n### Change 2: Apply the same adaptive logic to the ext variant\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 233-250\n\nApply the identical `MIN_BLOCKS` adaptive loop to `_poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_ext`. The ext variant is used for extension-field merkle trees. Same while loop, same MIN_BLOCKS constant.\n\n### No kernel changes required\n\nThe kernel itself (`poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel`) is unmodified. The per-thread computation is identical regardless of `blockDim.x`. The shared memory Merkle reduction (lines 53-71) uses `shared_offset = ((leaf_idx >> (layer+1)) << layer) * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x` which stays within bounds at any blockDim.x ≥ 1.\n\nThe `__syncthreads()` barriers (lines 61, 71) remain correct because they synchronize all threads within a block, and the shared memory accesses are bounded by `shared_stride = blockDim.x * (blockDim.y >> 1)`.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Each thread processes exactly one stride position (`stride_idx = blockDim.x * blockIdx.x + threadIdx.x`). Total thread count = grid.x × block.x × block.y = query_stride × rows_per_query = height. Changing blockDim.x only redistributes stride positions across blocks; per-thread computation is unchanged. The output is bit-identical.\n\n2. **APC 0 no-regression**: When query_stride is large (≥ default_bx × MIN_BLOCKS), the while loop exits immediately and the launch geometry is unchanged from today.\n\n3. **Shared memory bounds**: `shared_stride = block.x × div_ceil(block.y, 2)`. The reduction tree accesses at most `CELLS_OUT × shared_stride` Fp elements. With smaller block.x, shared_stride shrinks, but all indices (`shared_offset < shared_stride`) remain within bounds.\n\n4. **Thread count**: grid.x × block.x × block.y = query_stride × (1 << log_rows_per_query) = height. This is preserved regardless of block.x choice.\n\n5. **No VPMM pool state impact**: This change does not alter any GPU memory allocation patterns. It only changes kernel launch geometry. The VPMM pool state sensitivity (observed in multiple previous tasks) is not a concern.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Run `PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics metrics.json --recursion` for APC 0, 100, 300 before and after.\n2. Compare `STARK (excl. trace)` and `Trace Commit` via `spec.py`.\n3. Run nsight profile on APC 300 after the change to verify:\n   - `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` instance count is unchanged (25)\n   - Grid dimensions increased from 32 to 128 for trace commit instances\n   - Total kernel GPU time decreased (231ms → ~120-150ms expected)\n4. APC 0 must show no regression (within ±20ms noise on STARK excl trace).\n\n### Expected results\n\n- Trace Commit APC 300: 197ms → ~145-160ms (hash portion ~40ms/seg → ~13ms/seg)\n- STARK excl trace APC 300: 1101ms → ~1045-1065ms (~3-5% improvement, ~2.30x vs baseline)\n- APC 0: no change (2048 blocks already saturate 128 SMs)\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- Less than 20ms improvement on STARK excl trace at APC 300.\n- Any regression > 20ms on STARK excl trace at APC 0.\n- Any correctness failure (proof verification fails at any APC configuration).\n"}}
