{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QDXm2bNXvP8end8eAmAHGU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batch Stacked Reduction Round 0 Descriptor Arrays\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce kernel launch overhead in the stacked reduction Round 0 by batching per-trace kernel launches into per-height-group batched launches using descriptor arrays. At APC 300, the stacked reduction Round 0 calls `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` and `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` once per backing trace matrix (~400 per segment across all commits), with many being tiny (1-2 columns, height 2^5-2^8). By grouping same-height traces into single batched kernel launches, we reduce ~800 kernel launches per segment to ~80, saving ~720 launches × ~5µs = ~3.6ms per segment.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Round 0 sumcheck loop\n10\t\n11\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, lines 504-549\n12\t\n13\t```rust\n14\tfor ((trace_ptr, trace_height, trace_width), window) in zip(\n15\t    mem::take(&mut self.trace_ptrs),\n16\t    self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2),\n17\t) {\n18\t    let n = log_height as isize - l_skip as isize;\n19\t    let d_g_output = if n >= 0 { &mut d_g_pos } else { &mut d_g_neg[(-n-1) as usize] };\n20\t\n21\t    // Per-trace temp buffer\n22\t    if block_sums_len > self.d_block_sums.len() {\n23\t        self.d_block_sums = DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(block_sums_len);\n24\t    }\n25\t\n26\t    stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0(\n27\t        &self.eq_r_ns, trace_ptr, lambda_pows_ptr,\n28\t        &mut self.d_block_sums, d_g_output,\n29\t        trace_height, trace_width, l_skip,\n30\t    );\n31\t}\n32\t```\n33\t\n34\tEach call launches a CUDA kernel that computes G0, G1, G2 partial sums for one trace matrix, then a final reduction kernel that ADDs block sums into the output bucket.\n35\t\n36\t### PLE fold loop\n37\t\n38\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, lines 714-743\n39\t\n40\t```rust\n41\tfor trace in &stacked.traces {\n42\t    stacked_reduction_fold_ple(\n43\t        trace.buffer().as_ptr(), dst,\n44\t        &self.d_omega_skip_pows, &d_inv_lagrange_denoms,\n45\t        trace.height(), trace.width(), l_skip,\n46\t    );\n47\t    dst_offset += new_height * trace.width();\n48\t}\n49\t```\n50\t\n51\tEach call launches one CUDA kernel that folds a single trace's PLE evaluations.\n52\t\n53\t### CUDA kernel signatures\n54\t\n55\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`, lines 44-63\n56\t\n57\t```c\n58\t// Round 0: computes G0, G1, G2 block sums + final reduction\n59\t_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0(\n60\t    eq_r_ns, trace_ptr, lambda_pows, block_sums, output,\n61\t    trace_height, trace_width, l_skip, num_x);\n62\t\n63\t// PLE fold: folds trace evals by Lagrange interpolation at u_0\n64\t_stacked_reduction_fold_ple(\n65\t    src, dst, omega_skip_pows, inv_lagrange_denoms,\n66\t    trace_height, trace_width, l_skip);\n67\t```\n68\t\n69\t### nsight kernel data (APC 300)\n70\t\n71\t- `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel`: 22ms total, 797 instances, avg 28µs\n72\t- `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel`: 9ms total, 797 instances, avg 11µs\n73\t- Total: 31ms GPU, ~26ms wall clock (37ms + 36ms for 2 segments minus MLE portion)\n74\t- Per-segment round 0 wall clock: 14ms (seg0), 12ms (seg1) = 26ms total\n75\t- The ~400 launches per segment include traces from common_main + preprocessed + cached_main commits\n76\t\n77\t## Changes\n78\t\n79\t### Change 1: Add batched Round 0 CUDA kernel\n80\t\n81\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu` (or a new file `stacked_reduction_batched.cu`)\n82\t\n83\tAdd a descriptor struct and batched kernel:\n84\t\n85\t```c\n86\tstruct StackedR0Desc {\n87\t    const Fp *trace_ptr;\n88\t    const FpExt *lambda_pows;  // already offset to this trace's position\n89\t    uint32_t trace_width;\n90\t};\n91\t\n92\t// Batched kernel: processes all traces of the same height in one launch.\n93\t// Each block processes one trace in the descriptor array.\n94\t// Uses atomicAdd to accumulate into shared G output buffer.\n95\t__global__ void batched_stacked_reduction_round0_kernel(\n96\t    const FpExt *eq_r_ns,\n97\t    const StackedR0Desc *descs,\n98\t    const uint32_t *block_starts,  // prefix sum: which desc this block belongs to\n99\t    uint32_t num_descs,\n100\t    FpExt *block_sums,    // [total_blocks * NUM_G * skip_domain]\n101\t    uint32_t trace_height, // same for all in this batch\n102\t    uint32_t l_skip\n103\t);\n104\t```\n105\t\n106\tThe existing per-trace kernel body can be reused: each block computes partial G sums for its assigned trace, writing to its segment of block_sums. A final reduction kernel sums all block_sums into the G output.\n107\t\n108\t**Key design**: Use the `BlockCtx` binary-search pattern from the batch MLE kernel (`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`) to map blocks to descriptors.\n109\t\n110\t### Change 2: Add batched PLE fold CUDA kernel\n111\t\n112\tSame approach: descriptor array with per-trace src/dst pointers.\n113\t\n114\t```c\n115\tstruct FoldPleDesc {\n116\t    const Fp *src;        // trace buffer\n117\t    FpExt *dst;           // output buffer (already offset)\n118\t    uint32_t trace_width;\n119\t};\n120\t\n121\t__global__ void batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel(\n122\t    const FoldPleDesc *descs,\n123\t    const uint32_t *block_starts,\n124\t    uint32_t num_descs,\n125\t    const Fp *omega_skip_pows,\n126\t    const FpExt *inv_lagrange_denoms,\n127\t    uint32_t trace_height,\n128\t    uint32_t l_skip\n129\t);\n130\t```\n131\t\n132\t### Change 3: Add FFI bindings\n133\t\n134\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n135\t\n136\tAdd `repr(C)` structs for `StackedR0Desc` and `FoldPleDesc`, plus extern \"C\" declarations and safe wrapper functions.\n137\t\n138\t### Change 4: Orchestration in Rust\n139\t\n140\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`\n141\t\n142\tIn `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`:\n143\t1. Group `trace_ptrs` by (trace_height, sign_of_n) — traces that would accumulate into the same G buffer with the same kernel configuration.\n144\t2. For each group: build descriptor array on host, upload to device, launch batched kernel.\n145\t3. Use existing final_reduce pattern to accumulate block sums into G buffers.\n146\t\n147\tIn `fold_ple_evals`:\n148\t1. Group traces by trace_height.\n149\t2. For each group: build descriptor array, upload, launch batched kernel.\n150\t\n151\t**Threshold**: Only use batched path when group has ≥ 10 traces. Below that, the descriptor overhead exceeds the launch savings.\n152\t\n153\t### Change 5: Pre-allocate block_sums buffer\n154\t\n155\tCurrently, `self.d_block_sums` is resized per trace (line 529-531). With batched launches, pre-allocate a block_sums buffer large enough for the largest batch at the start of round 0.\n156\t\n157\t## Invariants\n158\t\n159\t1. **Correctness**: Each trace in the batch produces the same G0/G1/G2 partial sums as the per-trace path. The final reduction accumulates them identically. The bitwise output is identical to the sequential path (modulo floating point associativity, which doesn't apply to finite field arithmetic).\n160\t\n161\t2. **APC 0 no-regression**: At APC 0, there are ~20 traces per segment. Most groups will have < 10 traces, falling below the batching threshold. The sequential path runs unchanged.\n162\t\n163\t3. **Output buffer safety**: The round 0 batched kernel writes to block_sums (not directly to G output). The final reduce kernel adds block_sums into G output. This matches the current per-trace pattern where block_sums is a temporary buffer.\n164\t\n165\t4. **No VPMM pool impact**: The descriptor arrays are small (400 descriptors × 24 bytes = ~10 KB). The block_sums pre-allocation replaces per-trace resizing with a single upfront allocation. Net memory impact is negligible.\n166\t\n167\t5. **fold_ple output correctness**: Each trace's folded output is written to a non-overlapping region of the q_evals buffer (indexed by dst_offset). The batched kernel uses per-descriptor dst pointers that match the sequential per-trace offsets exactly.\n168\t\n169\t## Measurement Plan\n170\t\n171\t1. Run benchmark for APC 0, 100, 300 before and after the change.\n172\t2. Compare `prover.openings.stacked_reduction.round0_time_ms` per segment.\n173\t3. Compare `STARK (excl. trace)` overall.\n174\t4. Run nsight profile on APC 300 to verify:\n175\t   - `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel` instance count decreased (from ~797 to ~80-100)\n176\t   - Total kernel GPU time unchanged or slightly improved\n177\t   - No new kernel time regression in other phases\n178\t5. APC 0 must show no regression (within ±10ms noise).\n179\t\n180\t### Expected results\n181\t\n182\t- Stacked Reduction Round 0 per segment: 14ms → 10-12ms (seg0), 12ms → 9-11ms (seg1)\n183\t- STARK excl trace APC 300: 1101ms → ~1087-1093ms (8-14ms improvement)\n184\t- APC 0: no change (below batching threshold)\n185\t\n186\t## Rollback Criteria\n187\t\n188\t- Less than 5ms improvement on stacked reduction round 0 total at APC 300.\n189\t- Any regression > 10ms on STARK excl trace at APC 0.\n190\t- Any correctness failure (proof verification fails at any APC configuration).\n191\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01876r4u5FQGbTVf2wYoQs6e","session_id":"97849a12-7ea3-4743-9ec4-4f09ecc25100","uuid":"cd1d33fb-2fd0-4d60-8c6e-23fa13c6e29a","timestamp":"2026-04-16T14:28:43.614Z"}
